Pane fixtures for the screen reads in agent-adapters.sh (adp_turn_running / adp_turn_state /
adp_compacted).

PROVENANCE MATTERS MORE THAN USUAL HERE, because the anchor these pin is a claim about what real
clients render. Every file here is a verbatim capture of a real client TUI, taken at 100 columns,
except the one marked DERIVED below, which is built from a capture and says so. They came in two
batches, described in turn.

Machine- and person-specific text was replaced in every capture — rule zero: checkout and scratch paths,
usernames, the numeric UID, session identifiers, model identifiers and plan tiers, quota
percentages, and issue numbers. Nothing else was touched: the leading codepoints, the line
positions and where each marker sits are as captured, and those are the only properties the anchor
reads.

One substitution is worth naming because the first pass MISSED it and, at the time, the gate
could not help. A scratch path can carry the home directory HYPHEN-ENCODED rather than as a path,
which a scan for a literal "/Users/" or "/home/" does not see. The leak gate has since grown an
arm for that encoded form, so it now reds a committed instance too — but that arm is bounded, and
its bounds are stated beside it in scripts/check.sh ("Two limits taken deliberately"). Rule zero
covers every shape regardless ("not hashed or split up to hide it"). The builder still substitutes
the mangled form first and re-scans every fixture before writing; if you add a capture, check by
hand for the encodings those bounds leave out.

Why the anchor needs fixtures at all: a too-loose anchor restores the false `delivered` this
change exists to remove; a too-tight one makes a healthy running turn read as not-running, so the
commonest healthy path alarms and an operator learns to ignore the signal. Neither failure is
visible without real renders to measure against.

  pane-claude-running.txt        live, the first kind, mid-turn. Captured from a real supervised
                                 session's own pane over the agterm control socket. The turn
                                 marker is in the footer, which is the LAST non-empty line.
  pane-claude-idle.txt           live, same kind, idle at the composer. Same footer, no marker.
  pane-claude-draft.txt          live, same kind: a long directive TYPED BUT NOT SUBMITTED, so it
                                 wraps inside the composer. The marker is in the box, three lines
                                 above the footer. ADVERSARIAL: must read as not running.
  pane-claude-draft-welded.txt   live, same kind, the flattening case. Two fragments were typed as
                                 separate keystroke batches and the client rendered them as the
                                 intact marker inside the box — the shape `tr` welds together when
                                 a multi-line directive is flattened to one line. ADVERSARIAL.
                                 This is the case no earlier fixture had.
  pane-claude-queued.txt         live, same kind, a second message submitted while the first turn
                                 was still running. The client's queued hint is on the BOX line;
                                 the turn marker is simultaneously in the footer.
  pane-claude-content.txt        DERIVED, not a verbatim capture. Built from pane-claude-idle.txt
                                 by putting the marker into a line that frame already rendered as
                                 two-space-indented transcript text, and changing nothing else. It
                                 stands for a child displaying this plugin's own source, which is
                                 what permanently poisons a slot if the anchor is loose. The line's
                                 SHAPE is real; only its words are ours. ADVERSARIAL.
  pane-codex-running.txt         live, the second kind, mid-turn. This kind puts the marker in a
                                 column-one service line in the transcript flow, NOT in the
                                 footer — which is why the anchor needs two arms.
  pane-codex-idle.txt            live, same kind, idle at the composer.
  pane-codex-draft-welded.txt    live, same kind, typed-not-submitted and welded. ADVERSARIAL.
  pane-codex-queued.txt          live, same kind, a second message submitted mid-turn. This kind
                                 renders its queued header as a COLUMN-ONE SERVICE LINE, not in
                                 the composer — a different place from the first kind, which is
                                 why the queued arm has two anchors. Reasoning by analogy from one
                                 kind to the other would have got this wrong and made every
                                 mid-turn send on this kind alarm. That line also carries the turn
                                 marker, which is why `queued` outranks `running`.

A SECOND BATCH, taken later from throwaway sessions of both kinds in a detached tmux terminal at
100x40 (tmux capture-pane), each driven by a trivial prompt in an empty scratch directory. Same
substitutions as above, plus the model's own announcement tip and the wall-clock times. All of them
are verbatim; the screens derived from them are built inline in t-turn.sh, each marked DERIVED.

  pane-codex-ran-marker.txt      live, the second kind, IDLE after running a grep for the turn
                                 marker. The command's header renders in column one as a service
                                 line carrying the marker, which the bare-substring read called
                                 mid-turn. ADVERSARIAL: must read as not running.
  pane-codex-running-tail.txt    live, same kind, mid-turn with a background terminal. The client
                                 APPENDS text after its live-status tail on the same line — so the
                                 tail cannot be anchored at the end of the line.
  pane-codex-compacting.txt      live, same kind, just after `/compact`: its status line is up and
                                 nothing says done. The client echoes no submitted slash command.
  pane-codex-compacted.txt       live, same kind, finished: a column-one `Context compacted`
                                 service line, lower-case, is the last service line.
  pane-codex-compacted-stale.txt live, same kind, the finished screen followed by one more turn.
                                 The old completion line is still in the viewport.
  pane-claude-compacting.txt     live, the first kind, mid-compaction: the command's echo is a
                                 column-one glyph line and the turn marker is in the footer.
  pane-claude-compacted.txt      live, same kind, finished: an indented result line directly
                                 under that echo.
  pane-claude-compacted-stale.txt live, same kind, the finished screen followed by one more turn.

A THIRD BATCH, of the third kind (agy 1.2.13), taken the same way (tmux, 100x40, a scratch
repository, a trivial prompt). Substituted as above, plus the account line and the model name.
They pin what agy does NOT give the turn read, and they are not banner evidence (t-wait.sh says
why agy stays unanchored).

  pane-agy-running.txt           live, mid-turn: a spinner line and an `esc to cancel` footer.
  pane-agy-idle.txt              live, idle after the turn: `? for shortcuts` footer. Its
                                 `Thought for 3s, 552 tokens` line is per-turn, not a session
                                 total — the reason the ctx column reads nothing for agy.
  pane-agy-menu.txt              live, idle with the slash menu open: the SAME `esc to cancel`
                                 footer as mid-turn, so that footer is not a turn marker.

NOT ASSERTED: whether either kind ever renders its queued hint somewhere other than the place
captured here. A third placement, if one exists, reads as not-queued and falls to the alarm path.
